<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ahhiyawa</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Late Bronze Age power named in some thirty Hittite texts, widely identified with the Mycenaean Greeks, the Achaeans of Homer.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The name Ahhiyawa, close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-homer">Achaeans</a>, appears in Hittite records from c. 1400 BCE, when a 'man of Ahhiya' named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attarissiya">Attarissiya</a> raided western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-alashiya">Alashiya</a>. Most scholars now accept that it denotes a Mycenaean Greek kingdom, or a coalition of them, though its centre is debated, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mycenae">Mycenae</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> all proposed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 13th century BCE a Hittite king addressed the king of Ahhiyawa as a '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a>' and 'brother', ranking him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. Ahhiyawa held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/millawanda">Millawanda</a> on the Anatolian coast and backed rebels such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piyamaradu">Piyamaradu</a>. Hittite texts mention ships from Ahhiyawa and a dispute over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wilusa">Wilusa</a>, often linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In a treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv">Tudhaliya IV</a>, c. 1230 BCE, the king of Ahhiyawa was struck from the list of great kings, suggesting its power had waned. References cease soon after, as the Mycenaean palaces collapsed c. 1200 BCE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ahhiyawa.jpg" alt="Ahhiyawa" width="320" height="316"><figcaption>Image: Sosias (potter, signed). Painting attributed to the Sosias Painter (name piece for Beazley, overriding attribution) o... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akhilleus_Patroklos_Antikensammlung_Berlin_F2278.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ahhiyawan kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>

